🧩 1️⃣ OVERVIEW — WHAT IS HIERARCHY?
-----------------------------------
Hierarchy = The *tree structure* of how SystemVerilog design and testbench components are organized and connected.

Every `module`, `interface`, and `program` you instantiate adds a new level to the simulation hierarchy.

Think of it like a *family tree* of your entire design.

Example hierarchy tree:
    tb_top
     ├── dut
     │    ├── internal_block1
     │    └── internal_block2
     ├── if_inst
     └── driver, monitor, scoreboard  (class-based components)

💡 In DV context:
- DUT → actual hardware design
- Interface → signal bundle connecting DUT & TB
- TB / Class Hierarchy → environment that drives, monitors, and checks DUT

===============================================================================
🧠 2️⃣ WHY HIERARCHY MATTERS
----------------------------
| Area | Importance |
|-------|-------------|
| **Connectivity** | Defines how DUT, interfaces, and TB communicate |
| **Debugging** | Lets you reference any signal using its full path (e.g., tb.dut.data) |
| **Reuse** | Enables modular, maintainable TB structures |
| **UVM Integration** | Mirrors static hardware hierarchy with dynamic class hierarchy |

💬 In simulation, you can inspect hierarchy in the QuestaSim “Objects” or “Hierarchy” pane to trace all connections visually.

===============================================================================
🔹 3️⃣ SIMPLE EXAMPLE — DESIGN & TESTBENCH
------------------------------------------
module adder(input logic [3:0] a, b, output logic [4:0] sum);
  assign sum = a + b;
endmodule

module tb;
  logic [3:0] a, b;
  logic [4:0] sum;
  adder dut(.a(a), .b(b), .sum(sum));

  initial begin
    a = 3; b = 5;
    #10 $display("TB: a=%0d b=%0d sum=%0d", a, b, sum);
  end
endmodule

Hierarchy Tree:
    tb
     └── dut

Signals can be accessed as:
    tb.a, tb.dut.sum, tb.dut.a

===============================================================================
🔸 4️⃣ ADDING INTERFACES TO THE HIERARCHY
-----------------------------------------
interface adder_if;
  logic [3:0] a, b;
  logic [4:0] sum;
endinterface

module adder(adder_if aif);
  assign aif.sum = aif.a + aif.b;
endmodule

module tb;
  adder_if aif();
  adder dut(aif);

  initial begin
    aif.a = 3; aif.b = 4;
    #5 $display("Sum = %0d", aif.sum);
  end
endmodule

Hierarchy Tree:
    tb
     ├── aif (interface)
     │     ├── a
     │     ├── b
     │     └── sum
     └── dut

➡️ Interfaces appear as sub-branches in the hierarchy.

===============================================================================
🔹 5️⃣ HIERARCHICAL REFERENCES (DOT NOTATION)
---------------------------------------------
Any signal or variable can be accessed using its *full hierarchical path*:

    $display("%0d", tb.aif.a);
    $display("%0d", tb.dut.aif.sum);

✅ Valid for modules/interfaces/programs  
❌ Not valid for classes (they’re software objects, not hardware hierarchy)

===============================================================================
🔹 6️⃣ CLASS VS MODULE HIERARCHY
--------------------------------
| Type | Nature | Access Method | Example |
|-------|--------|---------------|----------|
| **Modules / Interfaces** | Static (hardware) | Hierarchical name | `tb.dut.signal` |
| **Classes** | Dynamic (software) | Object handle | `driver_obj.display()` |

Design Verification Engineers must understand both hierarchies, since testbench classes communicate with hardware through *virtual interfaces*.

===============================================================================
🔸 7️⃣ HIERARCHY + INTERFACE + CLASS CONNECTION
-----------------------------------------------
Typical DV architecture:
    tb_top
     ├── dut_if (interface)
     │    ├── modport drv_mp
     │    ├── modport mon_mp
     │    └── clocking block
     ├── dut
     └── env (class)
          ├── agent
          │     ├── driver (uses virtual interface)
          │     └── monitor (uses virtual interface)
          └── scoreboard

Class world (dynamic) connects to hardware world (static) using a **virtual interface handle**, e.g.:
    drv = new(tb_top.dut_if);

This creates the bridge between both hierarchies.

===============================================================================
🔹 8️⃣ WHY HIERARCHY IS ESSENTIAL FOR DV ENGINEERS
--------------------------------------------------
✅ **Signal tracing** — identify where a value originates  
✅ **Forcing signals** — apply overrides during debug (`force tb.dut.signal = 1'b1;`)  
✅ **Waveform viewing** — navigate simulation hierarchy in tools  
✅ **TB structure understanding** — modular connections (env → agent → driver)  
✅ **UVM organization** — runtime class hierarchy mirrors hardware hierarchy

===============================================================================
🧩 9️⃣ DV EXAMPLE
----------------------
module tb_top;
  logic clk;
  dut_if if_inst(clk);
  dut     dut_inst(if_inst);
  env     env_inst;   // class-based TB environment

  initial begin
    env_inst = new();
    env_inst.connect(if_inst);  // Connect virtual interface
    env_inst.run();             // Start simulation
  end
endmodule

Hierarchy View in Simulator:
    tb_top
     ├── clk
     ├── if_inst
     ├── dut_inst
     └── env_inst (class, dynamic)

