📗 INTERVIEW NOTES
-----------------------
1. What is SystemVerilog hierarchy?
   → Hierarchical structure of modules, interfaces, and programs that define how design and testbench elements are organized.

Q2. Why is hierarchy important in verification?
   → It enables connectivity, debugging, and modularity of the testbench and defines signal accessibility.

Q3. How is class hierarchy different from module hierarchy?
   → Class hierarchy is dynamic and uses object handles; module hierarchy is static and hardware-based.

Q4. How do you access a DUT signal from the testbench?
   → Using hierarchical reference like `tb_top.dut.signal`.

Q5. How do virtual interfaces relate to hierarchy?
   → Virtual interfaces use the *hierarchical instance path* to connect the class world with the design hierarchy.

===============================================================================
✅ KEY TAKEAWAYS
---------------------
• Hierarchy defines “who owns whom” in design & testbench.  
• Modules/interfaces form **static hierarchy**.  
• Classes/UVM components form **dynamic hierarchy**.  
• Virtual interfaces bridge both worlds.  
• Understanding hierarchy is critical for debugging and reusability.  

